<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The Mac App Store sandbox, and nothing speculative.

	  This is the entitlement set a submittable build needs. It is committed as a
	  real file rather than generated inside a recipe so the differences between
	  the two distribution routes stay reviewable: `make build` signs WITHOUT
	  these keys (Developer ID, unnotarized-sandbox, the GitHub/dmg route), and
	  `make deploy-sandbox` signs WITH them, which is the only way to find out
	  what the App Store build can actually do before paying a review cycle for
	  the answer.

	  Read docs/appstore/app-store-metadata.md §2 for the finding this exists to
	  reproduce: the sandbox is not a formality here, it decides whether the
	  agent-launching feature can ship at all.
	-->

	<!-- Required for every Mac App Store app. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The user picks the folder or volume to scan. Note that the scan itself
	     does NOT need this: Full Disk Access remains available to a sandboxed app
	     and is still granted by the user in System Settings, which is what makes
	     a whole-disk scan work. This key covers the picker. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Outbound HTTP to the model provider the user configured (a local Ollama
	     or LM Studio endpoint, or a hosted one). Without this the custom-provider
	     AI panel cannot connect at all. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The Rust scan engine is linked as a staticlib built outside Xcode, so the
	     binary loads code that is not signed by the app's own team. Required, not
	     optional: without it the app is killed at launch. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  DELIBERATELY ABSENT: com.apple.security.files.user-selected.executable.

	  That entitlement is the only proposed route to launching an external CLI
	  from a sandboxed app, and it was measured (2026-10-06): adding the key alone
	  changes NOTHING, because it only takes effect once the user grants it
	  through a file picker. It therefore cannot express "run whatever is on the
	  user's PATH", which is what the agent feature does, so it does not rescue
	  that feature and is not worth requesting. Adding it here would imply it
	  helps.

	  What the sandbox actually does to the app, measured with the shipping code
	  under this exact entitlement set: the user's login shell still runs, but it
	  sees a container filesystem, so `command -v claude` and `command -v codex`
	  return nothing and exec of an absolute path fails with "doesn't exist".
	  `xcrun` refuses outright ("cannot be used within an App Sandbox"). The
	  feature does not fail loudly — it silently finds no agents.

	  Use `make deploy-sandbox` to reproduce that, and `make deploy` to go back.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
